  "Number": 7,
  "Question": "For real numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b,\\) define \\(a \\$ b = (a - b)^2.\\) What is \\((x - y)^2 \\$ (y - x)^2?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(x^2 + y^2\\)",
    "C": "\\(2x^2\\)",
    "D": "\\(2y^2\\)",
    "E": "\\(4xy\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The useful observation is that reversing a subtraction changes its sign, but not its square. Since \\(y-x=-(x-y)\\), we have \\((y-x)^2=(x-y)^2\\).\n\nThe operation \\(a\\mathbin{\\$}b\\) means to subtract its two inputs and square the result. Thus\n\\[\n(x-y)^2\\mathbin{\\$}(y-x)^2\n=\\bigl((x-y)^2-(y-x)^2\\bigr)^2\n=0^2=0.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{0}\\), choice A.",
